~ core.net — networking primitives. Import with `<< core.net`; reach the primitive through
~ the `net` binding, `net.@tcpRequest(address, requestBytes)`. The HTTP client sits on this,
~ and user code may use the raw request-exchange primitive directly.

~ Perform a one-shot TCP request exchange: connect to `address` (`host:port`), write
~ `requestBytes`, then read the response until the peer closes the connection
~ (close-delimited).
~
~ Returns a `Result`: `Ok(responseBytes)` with the whole response as a Text on success, or
~ `NotOk(errorMessage)` on ANY network failure (DNS resolution, connect, write, or read) — the
~ message names the failing stage and the address. Network failure never crashes the program;
~ match the `Result` to handle it. The response is capped at 16 MiB; a larger one yields `NotOk`.
~ Hostname resolution runs on the runtime's blocking-call pool and parks only the calling fiber
~ until it answers; a numeric `host:port` resolves inline with no thread at all. See
~ docs/concurrency/runtime.md's "Blocking calls" section for the pool's sizing.
~
~ `@tcpRequest` is a leaf IO primitive (the `@` marker): calling it launches the exchange in
~ the background and hands back a DEFERRED Result immediately — the fiber only waits (forces)
~ once a strict operation reads it (a match, `print`, a native call, ...). The body below is an
~ inert placeholder — the call is compiler-lowered — but it pins BOTH payloads to `Text`, so a
~ caller's `Ok(bytes)` / `NotOk(error)` binding sees the concrete `Text`, not a generic payload.
>> @tcpRequest = (address :: Text, requestBytes :: Text) -> Result => <
  address == "" ? Ok("") : NotOk("")
>

~ A connection `net.@tcpServe` accepted, delivered fresh to `handler` on its own fiber. Its
~ one field is an opaque handle into the runtime's connection table; every member below is
~ compiler-lowered, so the bodies here are inert placeholders.
>> Connection = {
  handle :: Num,

  ~ The bytes that have arrived since the last read, as a deferred Text — "" once the peer
  ~ has closed. Parks on readiness and forces at the first strict use, like `@tcpRequest`.
  @read = () -> Text => < "" >,

  ~ Like `@read()`, but gives up and yields "" once `seconds` pass with nothing arriving —
  ~ the same "" a closed peer already yields, since neither carries a channel a Text result
  ~ could tell them apart through.
  @read = (seconds :: Num) -> Text => < "" >,

  ~ Write every byte of `bytes`, parking on writability until all of it is sent.
  @write = (bytes :: Text) -> $ => < $ >,

  ~ Close the connection. A handler that returns without calling this has its connection
  ~ closed by the runtime.
  close = () -> $ => < $ >
}

~ The address a `net.Server` actually bound, from `server.address()` — most useful after
~ binding port `0`, to learn which port the OS actually chose.
>> Address = {
  ~ The bound host, exactly as the OS reports it (`127.0.0.1`, `::1` — never bracketed).
  host :: Text,

  ~ The bound port, never `0` even when `@tcpServe` was asked for one.
  port :: Num,

  ~ `host:port`, exactly the form `@tcpRequest`/`@tcpServe` accept — an IPv6 `host` (one
  ~ containing `:`) wrapped in brackets.
  text = () -> Text => <
    it.host.contains(":") ? "[`it.host`]:`it.port`" : "`it.host`:`it.port`"
  >
}

~ A server `net.@tcpServe` started, returned the moment it begins listening.
>> Server = {
  handle :: Num,

  ~ Stop accepting, wait up to `seconds` for in-flight handlers to finish, then close any
  ~ connection still open and the listener itself. Parks the calling fiber until done.
  kill = (seconds :: Num) -> $ => < $ >,

  ~ `kill` with the default 5-second grace period.
  kill = () -> $ => < $ >,

  ~ The address this server actually bound — most useful after binding port `0` (any free
  ~ port), to learn which one the OS actually chose.
  address = () -> Address => < Address { host = "", port = 0 } >
}

~ Bind `address` (`host:port`, exactly what `@tcpRequest` accepts — a numeric IPv4/IPv6
~ address, an IPv6 literal in brackets, or a hostname resolved the same way, on the
~ runtime's blocking-call pool), listen, and return the `Server` handle at once — never
~ deferred. The accept loop runs as a launch of the calling block, so `^` stays alive
~ while the server runs; every accepted connection runs `handler` on its own fiber. A
~ bind failure (the address does not parse or resolve, the port is taken, or permission
~ is refused) is fatal.
>> @tcpServe = (address :: Text, handler :: (Connection) -> $) -> Server => <
  Server { handle = 0 }
>

~ The suite for `Address.text()`: offline over canned host/port pairs, so it runs anywhere.
~ Only `quilon test corelib/net.qn` compiles this block — every other command erases it.
<< core.test

test.describe("Address.text", () => <
  test.it("renders an IPv4 host:port with no brackets", () => <
    expect(Address { host = "127.0.0.1", port = 9047 }.text(), equals("127.0.0.1:9047"))
  >)

  test.it("wraps an IPv6 host in brackets", () => <
    expect(Address { host = "::1", port = 9048 }.text(), equals("[::1]:9048"))
  >)
>)
